<article class="env-lemma" id="0C36">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C36">Lemma <span data-tag="0C36">15.52.8</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Noetherian local ring which is a $P$-ring where $P$ satisfies (B), (C), (D), and (E). Then the henselization $R^ h$ and the strict henselization $R^{sh}$ are $P$-rings. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We have seen this for the henselization in Lemma <a href="/tag/0BKA" data-tag="0BKA">15.52.7</a>. To prove it for the strict henselization, it suffices to show that the formal fibres of $R^{sh}$ have $P$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0BIU" data-tag="0BIU">15.52.4</a>. Let $\mathfrak r \subset R^{sh}$ be a prime and set $\mathfrak p = R \cap \mathfrak r$. Set $\mathfrak r_1 = \mathfrak r$ and let $\mathfrak r_2, \ldots , \mathfrak r_ s$ be the other primes of $R^{sh}$ lying over $\mathfrak p$, so that $R^{sh} \otimes _ R \kappa (\mathfrak p) = \prod \nolimits _{i = 1, \ldots , s} \kappa (\mathfrak r_ i)$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/07QQ" data-tag="07QQ">15.46.13</a>. Then we see that 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \prod \nolimits _{i = 1, \ldots , t} (R^{sh})^\wedge \otimes _{R^{sh}} \kappa (\mathfrak r_ i) = (R^{sh})^\wedge \otimes _{R^{sh}} (R^{sh} \otimes _ R \kappa (\mathfrak p)) = (R^{sh})^\wedge \otimes _ R \kappa (\mathfrak p)  \]
</div>
    <p>
       Note that $R^\wedge \to (R^{sh})^\wedge $ is formally smooth in the $\mathfrak m_{(R^{sh})^\wedge }$-adic topology, see Lemma <a href="/tag/06LJ" data-tag="06LJ">15.46.3</a>. Hence $R^\wedge \to (R^{sh})^\wedge $ is regular by Proposition <a href="/tag/07PM" data-tag="07PM">15.50.2</a>. We conclude that property $P$ holds for $\kappa (\mathfrak p) \to (R^{sh})^\wedge \otimes _ R \kappa (\mathfrak p)$ by (C) and our assumption on $R$. Using property (B), using the decomposition above, and looking at local rings we conclude that property $P$ holds for $\kappa (\mathfrak p) \to (R^{sh})^\wedge \otimes _{R^{sh}} \kappa (\mathfrak r)$. Since $\kappa (\mathfrak r)/\kappa (\mathfrak p)$ is separable algebraic, it follows from (E) that $P$ holds for $\kappa (\mathfrak r) \to (R^{sh})^\wedge \otimes _{R^{sh}} \kappa (\mathfrak r)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>